1.Aim

To implement Matplotlib and Pandas libraries in python

2.Algorithm

1.Import Pandas and Matplotlib libraries.
2.Create a dictionary containing student names, marks and study hours.
3.Convert the dictionary into a DataFrame.
4.Display the DataFrame.
5.Draw a line chart using `plt.plot()`.
6.Draw a bar chart using `plt.bar()`.
7.Display the marks on the bar chart.
8.Draw a histogram using `plt.hist()`.
9.Calculate the mean value of marks.
10.Display the mean value using a vertical line.
11.Stop the program.

3.Source Code

import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
data = {
 'name': ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e'],
 'marks': [100, 70, None, 40, 50],
 'study_hours': [6, 2, 3, 1, 5]
}
df = pd.DataFrame(data)
print(df)
# Line chart
plt.plot(df['name'], df['marks'],
 marker='D', linestyle='--', linewidth=3)
plt.title('student marks chart')
plt.xlabel('students')
plt.ylabel('marks')
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()
# Bar chart
plt.bar(df['name'], df['marks'])
for i, val in enumerate(df['marks']):
    if pd.notna(val):
        plt.text(i, val, str(val),ha='center', va='bottom')
plt.title('student marks chart')
plt.xlabel('students')
plt.ylabel('marks')
plt.ylim(0, 100)
plt.grid(axis='y')
plt.show()
# Histogram
plt.hist(df['marks'], bins=5, edgecolor='black')
plt.title('marks distribution')
plt.xlabel('marks')
plt.ylabel('frequency')
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()
# Mean value
mean_value = df['marks'].mean()
plt.axvline(mean_value, color='r',
 linestyle='dashed', linewidth=2)
plt.show()

